hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well according to Jean  Paul Sartre in existentialism is a  humanism the word humanism itself has  multiple senses and he distinguishes two  main kinds of humanism this is something  that is very important to keep in mind  both generally most philosophical terms  tend to be rather polysemic or ambiguous  like like you know freedom or goodness  or anything like that they don't refer  just to one single thing they don't have  just one sense and humanism is a word  like that as well it's very important  not to blur different kinds of humanism  in to each other to conflate them to  assume that there's just one thing out  there that's actually going by that term  and so Sartre is actually addressing  some of his critics who said listen in  one of your early novels nausea you said  the humanists were all wrong you made  fun of a certain kind of humanist why  come back to humanism now I assume this  name for yourself and the movement of  philosophy that you're advocating and  Sartre has a good response he says  listen I  understand that there's all sorts of  things going by this name humanism  here's how existentialist humanism is  different from them so we have 